<result>No blocking defects found in 5355360. One test-coverage gap and two low-confidence clock edge cases are below. Verdict: **COMMENT**.

**Checks run**
- `npx tsc --noEmit` produced no errors.
- `node --experimental-strip-types --test src/targets/graphiti/target.test.ts` passed 57 of 57.

**(1) Time filter, `src/targets/graphiti/target.ts:263-273`, called at `:714` and `:727`**
- The harness reads `Date.now()` before it starts `pi`, and the child writes event `ts` using the same machine's clock. The `&gt;=` check keeps events stamped in the same millisecond as the start.
- `Date.parse` handles ISO strings with `Z` or `+08:00`. A `ts` it cannot parse becomes `NaN`, so the event is dropped. That makes the check fail with "found 0" rather than wrongly passing.
- [LOW, conf LOW] If the real extension writes `ts` to whole seconds, an event in the same second as the start rounds down below it and is dropped. That causes a false `graphiti_write_failed`. The fake writes milliseconds, and I found no real event stream on disk to check the extension's format.
- [LOW, conf LOW] A wall-clock step backwards (an NTP correction) during a run could drop real events. A `process_nonce` filter would avoid both of these issues, if the extension can report its nonce.

**(2) Wrong inclusion or exclusion**
- Ingests run one at a time (the mutex test passes), and the earlier `pi` process has exited before the next one starts, so earlier events are excluded.
- Retries and resumed runs on the same home get the same benefit.
- Only an orphaned child of a timed-out earlier run that keeps writing could leak events in. I consider that unlikely.

**(3) Does the test fail without the fix?** Only because the import of `selectInvocationWriteEvents` would fail.
- [MEDIUM, conf HIGH] `target.test.ts:38-53` tests the helper alone. If `:727` went back to the old filter, every test would still pass. Add a fake-pi integration test that ingests the same session id twice into one home.

**(4) Effect on `assertGraphitiWritesComplete`** — none; the function is unchanged and only receives fewer events. A repeated session that writes nothing now correctly takes the admission-drop path from 716df47, using this run's own stats. Before, it was judged on the first occurrence's events.</result>
